addLang('es', {
  'The Eleven Hands': 'Las once manos',
  'The Scribe did not draw every people with the same hand. Frost and pine came from one, grass and horses from another, bamboo and mist, monsoon and river, savanna and drum, sand and stars, birch and snowfields, cherry and mountain, island and tide, olive and sea, terrace and condor. That is why no two peoples see the world in the same way, and why each is right.': 'El Escriba no dibujó a todos los pueblos con la misma mano. De una surgieron la escarcha y los pinos; de otra, la hierba y los caballos; luego el bambú y la niebla, el monzón y el río, la sabana y el tambor, la arena y las estrellas, los abedules y los campos nevados, los cerezos y la montaña, las islas y las mareas, los olivos y el mar, las terrazas y el cóndor. Por eso ningún pueblo ve el mundo del mismo modo y todos tienen razón.',
  'The Empire of the Quill': 'El Imperio de la Pluma',
  'Long before the eleven crowns, one empire spoke for the whole page. Its scholars wrote laws in a script every people could read, and its roads ran from the ice to the desert. These broken walls are what remains of it. The empire called itself the Quill, because it believed it could finish what the Scribe began.': 'Mucho antes de las once coronas, un imperio hablaba en nombre de toda la página. Sus eruditos escribían leyes en una escritura que todos podían leer, y sus caminos iban del hielo al desierto. Estos muros derruidos son todo lo que queda. El imperio se llamaba la Pluma porque creía que podía terminar lo que el Escriba había comenzado.',
  'The Great Blot': 'La Gran Mancha',
  'In the reign of the last Quill Emperor, the scholars tried to write a new land into the world. The ink would not obey. It spilled across the page in a single night, black and wet, and where it dried the land was changed. The chronicles call that night the Great Blot. Most of the chronicles end there.': 'Durante el reinado del último emperador de la Pluma, los eruditos intentaron escribir una nueva tierra en el mundo. La tinta no obedeció. En una sola noche se derramó por la página, negra y húmeda, y donde se secó la tierra cambió. Las crónicas llaman a esa noche la Gran Mancha. La mayoría termina ahí.',
  'Ink Given Fear': 'La tinta hecha miedo',
  'The Blot has no shape of its own. It takes the shape of what people fear most. In the north it rose as the restless dead, in the islands as the aswang, in the forests as the leshy, in the desert as the ghoul. Each people met the monsters of its own stories. Do not mock another land’s legends. Here, legends bite.': 'La Mancha no tiene forma propia: adopta aquello que más teme la gente. En el norte surgió como los muertos inquietos; en las islas, como el aswang; en los bosques, como el leshy; en el desierto, como el ghoul. Cada pueblo se enfrentó a los monstruos de sus propias historias. No te burles de las leyendas de otras tierras. Aquí las leyendas muerden.',
  'The Sealing of the Deep Places': 'El sellado de las profundidades',
  'The strongest creatures of the Blot could not be killed, so the last mages of the Quill sealed them underground: in barrows, caves, tombs and sunken temples, one in every land. The seals were written to last a thousand years. Some say it has been longer than that.': 'Las criaturas más poderosas de la Mancha no podían morir, así que los últimos magos de la Pluma las sellaron bajo tierra: en túmulos, cuevas, tumbas y templos sumergidos, uno en cada tierra. Los sellos debían durar mil años. Algunos dicen que ya ha pasado más tiempo.',
  'The Fall of Scriptoria': 'La caída de Scriptoria',
  'The Quill capital, Scriptoria, stood at the centre of the world, where all the lands meet. When the Blot came, the city was drowned in ink. Nothing of it can be seen now, but travellers who stand at the heart of the world say the ground is warmer there, and the grass grows in straight lines, like handwriting.': 'Scriptoria, capital de la Pluma, se alzaba en el centro del mundo, donde se encuentran todas las tierras. Cuando llegó la Mancha, la ciudad quedó sumergida en tinta. Ya no se ve nada de ella, pero los viajeros que llegan al corazón del mundo dicen que el suelo es más cálido y la hierba crece en líneas rectas, como la escritura.',
  'The Eleven Crowns': 'Las once coronas',
  'After the fall, the survivors gathered under eleven banners, one for each of the Scribe’s hands. They swore never to let a single empire rule the page again. They have kept that oath, mostly by fighting one another. Alliances are signed and torn, and borders are drawn in dotted lines because no one trusts them to stay.': 'Tras la caída, los supervivientes se reunieron bajo once estandartes, uno por cada mano del Escriba. Juraron no permitir que un solo imperio gobernara la página de nuevo. Han cumplido el juramento, sobre todo luchando entre sí. Las alianzas se firman y se rompen; las fronteras se trazan con puntos porque nadie confía en que permanezcan.',
  'The Hunters’ Oath': 'El juramento de los cazadores',
  'Carved above a doorway: “We hunt what crawls out of the Blot, in every land and under every banner. We take no side in the quarrels of crowns. A monster slain in an enemy’s field is still a monster slain.” This was the first hall of the Hunters’ Lodge.': 'Tallado sobre una puerta: «Cazamos lo que sale de la Mancha, en toda tierra y bajo cualquier estandarte. No tomamos partido en las disputas de las coronas. Un monstruo abatido en el campo de un enemigo sigue siendo un monstruo abatido». Este fue el primer salón del Pabellón de Cazadores.',
  'Ink in the Blood': 'Tinta en la sangre',
  'When a great creature of the Blot is destroyed, its ink does not vanish. Some of it passes into the one who struck it down. That is where the old powers come from: frost, fire, the roar, the stomp of a titan. The Quill forbade such power. The Quill is gone.': 'Cuando una gran criatura de la Mancha muere, su tinta no desaparece. Una parte pasa a quien la derrotó. De ahí vienen los antiguos poderes: la escarcha, el fuego, el rugido y la pisada de un titán. La Pluma prohibía ese poder. La Pluma ya no existe.',
  'A Mapmaker’s Letter': 'Carta de un cartógrafo',
  '“I have walked from the frozen north to the island coasts, and still my map is not finished. Every time I return to a place, a path has moved or a ruin has appeared where there was none. I begin to think the world is still being drawn, and that we are walking on wet ink.”': '«He recorrido el norte helado y las costas de las islas, y mi mapa sigue sin terminar. Cada vez que vuelvo a un lugar, algún camino ha cambiado o aparece una ruina donde antes no había nada. Empiezo a pensar que el mundo aún se está dibujando y que caminamos sobre tinta fresca».',
  'The Edge of the Page': 'El borde de la página',
  'Sail far enough in any direction and the sea grows pale, then white. Sailors who came back said the water simply stops being drawn. The Quill scholars believed the edge of the world is not an end but a margin, waiting for someone to write in it.': 'Navega lo bastante lejos en cualquier dirección y el mar palidece hasta volverse blanco. Los marineros que regresaron dijeron que, sencillamente, el agua deja de dibujarse. Los eruditos de la Pluma creían que el borde del mundo no es un final, sino un margen que espera a que alguien escriba en él.',
  'The Dotted Borders': 'Las fronteras punteadas',
  'An old boundary stone. One side reads “Here ends the land of one crown.” The other side reads “Here begins the land of another.” Someone has scratched beneath both: “The grass does not know the difference.”': 'Una antigua piedra fronteriza. En un lado dice: «Aquí termina la tierra de una corona». En el otro: «Aquí comienza la tierra de otra». Alguien grabó debajo de ambas frases: «La hierba no conoce la diferencia».',
  'The Last Page': 'La última página',
  'The final stone is cracked through the middle. What can still be read says that the First Scribe left one page unwritten, hidden somewhere in the world. Whoever finds it, and holds a pen steady enough, will write how this story ends.': 'La última piedra está partida por la mitad. Lo que aún puede leerse dice que el Primer Escriba dejó una página sin escribir, escondida en algún lugar del mundo. Quien la encuentre y sostenga la pluma con firmeza decidirá cómo termina esta historia.'
});
addLang('pt', {
  'The Eleven Hands': 'As Onze Mãos',
  'The Scribe did not draw every people with the same hand. Frost and pine came from one, grass and horses from another, bamboo and mist, monsoon and river, savanna and drum, sand and stars, birch and snowfields, cherry and mountain, island and tide, olive and sea, terrace and condor. That is why no two peoples see the world in the same way, and why each is right.': 'O Escriba não desenhou todos os povos com a mesma mão. O gelo e os pinheiros vieram de uma, a relva e os cavalos de outra; depois o bambu e a névoa, a monção e o rio, a savana e o tambor, a areia e as estrelas, as bétulas e os campos nevados, as cerejeiras e a montanha, as ilhas e as marés, as oliveiras e o mar, os terraços e o condor. Por isso, nenhum povo vê o mundo da mesma maneira — e todos têm razão.',
  'The Empire of the Quill': 'O Império da Pena',
  'Long before the eleven crowns, one empire spoke for the whole page. Its scholars wrote laws in a script every people could read, and its roads ran from the ice to the desert. These broken walls are what remains of it. The empire called itself the Quill, because it believed it could finish what the Scribe began.': 'Muito antes das onze coroas, um império falava por toda a página. Os seus estudiosos escreviam leis numa escrita que todos os povos podiam ler, e as suas estradas iam do gelo ao deserto. Estas muralhas em ruínas são tudo o que resta. O império chamava-se Pena porque acreditava poder terminar o que o Escriba começara.',
  'The Great Blot': 'A Grande Mancha',
  'In the reign of the last Quill Emperor, the scholars tried to write a new land into the world. The ink would not obey. It spilled across the page in a single night, black and wet, and where it dried the land was changed. The chronicles call that night the Great Blot. Most of the chronicles end there.': 'No reinado do último Imperador da Pena, os estudiosos tentaram escrever uma nova terra no mundo. A tinta não obedeceu. Numa única noite, espalhou-se pela página, negra e húmida, e a terra mudou onde secou. As crónicas chamam a essa noite a Grande Mancha. A maioria das crónicas termina aí.',
  'Ink Given Fear': 'A tinta ganha forma de medo',
  'The Blot has no shape of its own. It takes the shape of what people fear most. In the north it rose as the restless dead, in the islands as the aswang, in the forests as the leshy, in the desert as the ghoul. Each people met the monsters of its own stories. Do not mock another land’s legends. Here, legends bite.': 'A Mancha não tem forma própria. Assume a forma daquilo que as pessoas mais temem. No norte, surgiu como os mortos inquietos; nas ilhas, como o aswang; nas florestas, como o leshy; no deserto, como o ghoul. Cada povo enfrentou os monstros das suas próprias histórias. Não troces das lendas de outras terras. Aqui, as lendas mordem.',
  'The Sealing of the Deep Places': 'O selamento das profundezas',
  'The strongest creatures of the Blot could not be killed, so the last mages of the Quill sealed them underground: in barrows, caves, tombs and sunken temples, one in every land. The seals were written to last a thousand years. Some say it has been longer than that.': 'As criaturas mais fortes da Mancha não podiam ser mortas, por isso os últimos magos da Pena selaram-nas no subsolo: em túmulos, cavernas, criptas e templos submersos, um em cada terra. Os selos deviam durar mil anos. Alguns dizem que já passou mais tempo.',
  'The Fall of Scriptoria': 'A queda de Scriptoria',
  'The Quill capital, Scriptoria, stood at the centre of the world, where all the lands meet. When the Blot came, the city was drowned in ink. Nothing of it can be seen now, but travellers who stand at the heart of the world say the ground is warmer there, and the grass grows in straight lines, like handwriting.': 'Scriptoria, capital da Pena, ficava no centro do mundo, onde todas as terras se encontram. Quando a Mancha chegou, a cidade foi submersa em tinta. Nada dela se vê agora, mas os viajantes que chegam ao coração do mundo dizem que o chão é mais quente e a relva cresce em linhas direitas, como uma caligrafia.',
  'The Eleven Crowns': 'As Onze Coroas',
  'After the fall, the survivors gathered under eleven banners, one for each of the Scribe’s hands. They swore never to let a single empire rule the page again. They have kept that oath, mostly by fighting one another. Alliances are signed and torn, and borders are drawn in dotted lines because no one trusts them to stay.': 'Depois da queda, os sobreviventes reuniram-se sob onze estandartes, um por cada mão do Escriba. Juraram nunca mais deixar um único império governar a página. Cumpriram esse juramento, sobretudo lutando entre si. As alianças são assinadas e rasgadas; as fronteiras são pontilhadas porque ninguém confia que permaneçam.',
  'The Hunters’ Oath': 'O juramento dos caçadores',
  'Carved above a doorway: “We hunt what crawls out of the Blot, in every land and under every banner. We take no side in the quarrels of crowns. A monster slain in an enemy’s field is still a monster slain.” This was the first hall of the Hunters’ Lodge.': 'Gravado acima de uma porta: «Caçamos o que rasteja para fora da Mancha, em todas as terras e sob qualquer estandarte. Não tomamos partido nas disputas das coroas. Um monstro abatido no campo de um inimigo continua a ser um monstro abatido.» Este foi o primeiro salão do Pavilhão dos Caçadores.',
  'Ink in the Blood': 'Tinta no sangue',
  'When a great creature of the Blot is destroyed, its ink does not vanish. Some of it passes into the one who struck it down. That is where the old powers come from: frost, fire, the roar, the stomp of a titan. The Quill forbade such power. The Quill is gone.': 'Quando uma grande criatura da Mancha é destruída, a sua tinta não desaparece. Parte dela passa para quem a derrotou. É daí que vêm os antigos poderes: o gelo, o fogo, o rugido, o passo de um titã. A Pena proibia esse poder. A Pena já não existe.',
  'A Mapmaker’s Letter': 'Carta de um cartógrafo',
  '“I have walked from the frozen north to the island coasts, and still my map is not finished. Every time I return to a place, a path has moved or a ruin has appeared where there was none. I begin to think the world is still being drawn, and that we are walking on wet ink.”': '«Percorri o norte gelado e as costas das ilhas, mas o meu mapa ainda não está completo. Sempre que volto a um lugar, um caminho mudou ou surgiu uma ruína onde nada havia. Começo a pensar que o mundo ainda está a ser desenhado e que caminhamos sobre tinta fresca.»',
  'The Edge of the Page': 'A margem da página',
  'Sail far enough in any direction and the sea grows pale, then white. Sailors who came back said the water simply stops being drawn. The Quill scholars believed the edge of the world is not an end but a margin, waiting for someone to write in it.': 'Navega o suficiente em qualquer direção e o mar empalidece, depois fica branco. Os marinheiros que regressaram disseram que a água simplesmente deixa de ser desenhada. Os estudiosos da Pena acreditavam que o limite do mundo não era um fim, mas uma margem à espera de ser escrita.',
  'The Dotted Borders': 'As fronteiras pontilhadas',
  'An old boundary stone. One side reads “Here ends the land of one crown.” The other side reads “Here begins the land of another.” Someone has scratched beneath both: “The grass does not know the difference.”': 'Uma antiga pedra de fronteira. De um lado lê-se «Aqui termina a terra de uma coroa». Do outro, «Aqui começa a terra de outra». Alguém gravou por baixo: «A relva não sabe a diferença».',
  'The Last Page': 'A última página',
  'The final stone is cracked through the middle. What can still be read says that the First Scribe left one page unwritten, hidden somewhere in the world. Whoever finds it, and holds a pen steady enough, will write how this story ends.': 'A última pedra está rachada ao meio. O que ainda se consegue ler diz que o Primeiro Escriba deixou uma página por escrever, escondida algures no mundo. Quem a encontrar e tiver mão firme para segurar a pena escreverá o final desta história.'
});
addLang('ja', {
  'A narrow passage. Your footsteps echo a long way.': '狭い通路。足音が遠くまで響いていく。', 'Old flagstones, worn smooth by feet that are long gone.': '古い敷石は、とうに去った人々の足で滑らかにすり減っている。', 'Pillars carved like reeds hold up a low ceiling.': '葦のような彫刻が施された柱が、低い天井を支えている。', 'A dry fountain. Something once lived in the basin.': '水のない噴水。かつては水盤に何かが住んでいた。', 'Shelves of scrolls, rotted to black paste.': '巻物の棚。腐り果てて黒い泥になっている。', 'Scratches on the wall. Someone counted days here.': '壁の引っかき傷。誰かがここで日数を数えていた。', 'Rusted spears lean against the wall of an old guard post.': '古い詰め所の壁に、錆びた槍が立てかけられている。', 'The ceiling is lost in the dark above you.': '天井は頭上の闇に消えている。', 'Black ink seeps between the stones and whispers when you step on it.': '石の隙間から黒い墨が染み出し、踏むと囁き声を上げる。', 'A cold draught, and the smell of wet stone.': '冷たい隙間風と、濡れた石の匂い。',
  'A tile sinks under your foot and darts hiss out of the walls': '足元の石板が沈み、壁から矢が音を立てて飛び出した。', 'A wire snaps and a blade swings down from the ceiling': '糸が切れ、天井から刃が振り下ろされた。', 'The floor gives way and you fall into a pit': '床が崩れ、穴へ落ちた。', 'Black ink-gas pours from the cracks and fills your head with screams': '黒い墨のガスが裂け目から噴き出し、頭の中を悲鳴で満たした。', 'The ceiling groans, and stones rain down on you': '天井がきしみ、石が雨のように降り注いだ。',
  'A voice in the wall': '壁の中の声', 'A voice speaks from inside the stone. “Tell me your name, little hunter. I have forgotten mine.”': '石の中から声がする。「小さな狩人よ、君の名を教えてくれ。私は自分の名を忘れてしまった。」', 'Tell it your name': '自分の名を告げる', 'Say nothing': '何も言わない',
  'A lost child': '迷子の子ども', 'A small child in old clothes stands in the fog, holding out a hand. “I know the way out. Come.”': '古びた服を着た小さな子どもが霧の中に立ち、手を差し出した。「出口を知ってるよ。おいで。」', 'Follow the child': '子どもについていく', 'Do not follow': 'ついていかない',
  'An old pantry': '古い食料庫', 'Sealed clay jars line a shelf. The wax on the lids is still whole.': '封をした土器が棚に並ぶ。蓋の蝋はまだ完全に残っている。', 'Open the jars': '壺を開ける', 'Leave them': 'そのままにする',
  'A hunters’ camp': '狩人の野営地', 'A cold fire, three bedrolls and packs nobody came back for.': '消えた焚き火、三つの寝袋、誰も取りに戻らなかった荷物。', 'Take what you can': '使えそうな物を取る',
  'A mirror of ink': '墨の鏡', 'A pool of still ink on the floor shows your face, but older, scarred, and very tired.': '床の静かな墨だまりに、年老いて傷だらけで疲れ切った自分の顔が映る。', 'Look closer': '近くで見る', 'Look away': '目をそらす',
  'A collapsed tunnel': '崩落した坑道', 'The roof has come down here. Through a gap you see the glint of metal.': 'ここは天井が崩れている。隙間から金属の光が見えた。', 'Dig through': '掘り進む', 'Leave it': '諦める',
  'A prisoner in chains': '鎖につながれた囚人', 'A thin man hangs in rusted chains, half turned to ink. “Water… please…”': '痩せた男が錆びた鎖に吊られ、半ば墨に変わっている。「水を……頼む……」', 'Give him food': '食べ物を渡す',
  'Hunters’ marks': '狩人の印', 'Someone has scratched a message into the floor: <i>“Turn back. Or go on, but not hungry.”</i>': '誰かが床に言葉を刻んでいた。<i>「引き返せ。進むなら、腹を満たしてから。」</i>', 'Read on': '読み進める'
});
addLang('zh', {
  'A narrow passage. Your footsteps echo a long way.': '狭窄的通道里，你的脚步声传向远方。', 'Old flagstones, worn smooth by feet that are long gone.': '古老的石板被早已离去的人们踩得光滑。', 'Pillars carved like reeds hold up a low ceiling.': '雕成芦苇模样的石柱支撑着低矮的天花板。', 'A dry fountain. Something once lived in the basin.': '一座干涸的喷泉。水池里曾经住过什么。', 'Shelves of scrolls, rotted to black paste.': '架上的卷轴已经腐烂成黑色糊状物。', 'Scratches on the wall. Someone counted days here.': '墙上有抓痕。有人曾在这里数日子。', 'Rusted spears lean against the wall of an old guard post.': '锈蚀的长矛靠在旧岗哨的墙边。', 'The ceiling is lost in the dark above you.': '头顶的天花板消失在黑暗之中。', 'Black ink seeps between the stones and whispers when you step on it.': '黑墨从石缝中渗出，你踩上去时，它低声呢喃。', 'A cold draught, and the smell of wet stone.': '一阵冷风，还有潮湿石头的气味。',
  'A tile sinks under your foot and darts hiss out of the walls': '脚下的石板下陷，墙中嗖嗖射出飞镖。', 'A wire snaps and a blade swings down from the ceiling': '细线崩断，一把刀刃从天花板上摆落。', 'The floor gives way and you fall into a pit': '地面塌陷，你跌入坑中。', 'Black ink-gas pours from the cracks and fills your head with screams': '黑色墨气从裂缝涌出，尖叫声充满你的脑海。', 'The ceiling groans, and stones rain down on you': '天花板发出呻吟，石块如雨般砸向你。',
  'A voice in the wall': '墙中的声音', 'A voice speaks from inside the stone. “Tell me your name, little hunter. I have forgotten mine.”': '石头深处传来声音：“小猎人，告诉我你的名字。我已经忘了自己的名字。”', 'Tell it your name': '告诉它你的名字', 'Say nothing': '保持沉默',
  'A lost child': '迷路的孩子', 'A small child in old clothes stands in the fog, holding out a hand. “I know the way out. Come.”': '一个穿着旧衣的小孩站在雾中，向你伸出手。“我知道出去的路。跟我来。”', 'Follow the child': '跟着孩子', 'Do not follow': '不跟着它',
  'An old pantry': '旧储藏室', 'Sealed clay jars line a shelf. The wax on the lids is still whole.': '架子上排着密封的陶罐，盖上的蜡封完好无损。', 'Open the jars': '打开陶罐', 'Leave them': '不去碰它们',
  'A hunters’ camp': '猎人营地', 'A cold fire, three bedrolls and packs nobody came back for.': '熄灭的火堆、三卷铺盖，还有几包无人回来取走的行囊。', 'Take what you can': '能拿的都拿走',
  'A mirror of ink': '墨镜', 'A pool of still ink on the floor shows your face, but older, scarred, and very tired.': '地面上一滩静止的墨映出你的脸，却苍老、满是伤痕，疲惫不堪。', 'Look closer': '仔细查看', 'Look away': '移开视线',
  'A collapsed tunnel': '坍塌的隧道', 'The roof has come down here. Through a gap you see the glint of metal.': '这里的顶棚塌了。从一道缝隙中，你看见金属的微光。', 'Dig through': '挖开通道', 'Leave it': '放弃',
  'A prisoner in chains': '锁链中的囚徒', 'A thin man hangs in rusted chains, half turned to ink. “Water… please…”': '一个瘦弱的男人吊在锈链上，半身已化作墨迹。“水……求你……”', 'Give him food': '给他食物',
  'Hunters’ marks': '猎人的刻痕', 'Someone has scratched a message into the floor: <i>“Turn back. Or go on, but not hungry.”</i>': '有人在地面刻下一句话：<i>“回头吧。若要继续，先填饱肚子。”</i>', 'Read on': '继续阅读'
});
addLang('ja', {
  'The Eleven Hands': '十一の手',
  'The Scribe did not draw every people with the same hand. Frost and pine came from one, grass and horses from another, bamboo and mist, monsoon and river, savanna and drum, sand and stars, birch and snowfields, cherry and mountain, island and tide, olive and sea, terrace and condor. That is why no two peoples see the world in the same way, and why each is right.': '書記はすべての民を同じ手で描いたわけではない。ある手は霜と松を、別の手は草原と馬を描き、竹と霧、モンスーンと川、サバンナと太鼓、砂と星、白樺と雪原、桜と山、島と潮、オリーブと海、段々畑とコンドルを描いた。だから、世界の見え方は民ごとに異なり、誰もが正しい。',
  'The Empire of the Quill': '羽根ペン帝国',
  'Long before the eleven crowns, one empire spoke for the whole page. Its scholars wrote laws in a script every people could read, and its roads ran from the ice to the desert. These broken walls are what remains of it. The empire called itself the Quill, because it believed it could finish what the Scribe began.': '十一の王冠よりはるか昔、一つの帝国が世界全体を代表していた。その学者たちは誰もが読める文字で法を書き、氷原から砂漠まで道を通した。この崩れた壁は、その名残だ。書記の始めた仕事を自ら完結できると信じ、帝国は羽根ペンを名乗った。',
  'The Great Blot': '大いなる墨の染み',
  'In the reign of the last Quill Emperor, the scholars tried to write a new land into the world. The ink would not obey. It spilled across the page in a single night, black and wet, and where it dried the land was changed. The chronicles call that night the Great Blot. Most of the chronicles end there.': '最後の羽根ペン皇帝の時代、学者たちは新たな土地を世界に書き加えようとした。墨は言うことを聞かず、一夜にして黒く濡れたままページに広がった。乾いた場所では大地が変わっていた。年代記はその夜を「大いなる墨の染み」と呼ぶ。多くの年代記はそこで終わる。',
  'Ink Given Fear': '恐怖を得た墨',
  'The Blot has no shape of its own. It takes the shape of what people fear most. In the north it rose as the restless dead, in the islands as the aswang, in the forests as the leshy, in the desert as the ghoul. Each people met the monsters of its own stories. Do not mock another land’s legends. Here, legends bite.': '墨の染みには固有の姿がない。人々が最も恐れるものの形を取る。北ではさまよう死者、島々ではaswang、森ではleshy、砂漠ではghoulとなって現れた。どの民も、自らの物語に登場する怪物と向き合った。他の土地の伝説を笑ってはいけない。ここでは伝説が牙をむく。',
  'The Sealing of the Deep Places': '深淵の封印',
  'The strongest creatures of the Blot could not be killed, so the last mages of the Quill sealed them underground: in barrows, caves, tombs and sunken temples, one in every land. The seals were written to last a thousand years. Some say it has been longer than that.': '墨の染みの最強の怪物たちは倒せなかった。そこで羽根ペン帝国最後の魔術師たちは、それらを各地の地下に封じた。塚、洞窟、墓、海底神殿――それぞれの土地に一体ずつ。封印は千年もつよう記された。すでにそれ以上が過ぎたと言う者もいる。',
  'The Fall of Scriptoria': 'Scriptoriaの陥落',
  'The Quill capital, Scriptoria, stood at the centre of the world, where all the lands meet. When the Blot came, the city was drowned in ink. Nothing of it can be seen now, but travellers who stand at the heart of the world say the ground is warmer there, and the grass grows in straight lines, like handwriting.': '羽根ペン帝国の都Scriptoriaは、すべての土地が交わる世界の中心にあった。墨の染みが現れると、街は墨に沈んだ。今では何も見えないが、世界の中心に立った旅人は、地面が温かく、草が文字のようにまっすぐ生えていると言う。',
  'The Eleven Crowns': '十一の王冠',
  'After the fall, the survivors gathered under eleven banners, one for each of the Scribe’s hands. They swore never to let a single empire rule the page again. They have kept that oath, mostly by fighting one another. Alliances are signed and torn, and borders are drawn in dotted lines because no one trusts them to stay.': '帝国の滅亡後、生き残った人々は書記の十一の手にちなむ旗の下に集まった。一つの帝国に再び世界を支配させないと誓った。その誓いは、主に互いに争うことで守られてきた。同盟は結ばれては破られ、国境は点線で引かれる。誰も境界が続くとは信じていないからだ。',
  'The Hunters’ Oath': '狩人たちの誓い',
  'Carved above a doorway: “We hunt what crawls out of the Blot, in every land and under every banner. We take no side in the quarrels of crowns. A monster slain in an enemy’s field is still a monster slain.” This was the first hall of the Hunters’ Lodge.': '戸口の上に刻まれた言葉。「我らはあらゆる土地、あらゆる旗の下で、墨の染みから這い出るものを狩る。王冠同士の争いに味方はしない。敵の地で倒した怪物も、倒した怪物に変わりはない。」これが狩人の館の最初の広間だった。',
  'Ink in the Blood': '血に宿る墨',
  'When a great creature of the Blot is destroyed, its ink does not vanish. Some of it passes into the one who struck it down. That is where the old powers come from: frost, fire, the roar, the stomp of a titan. The Quill forbade such power. The Quill is gone.': '墨の染みの強大な怪物が倒されても、その墨は消えない。一部は倒した者へと受け継がれる。霜、炎、咆哮、巨人の踏み鳴らし――古の力はそこから生まれる。羽根ペン帝国はその力を禁じた。帝国はもうない。',
  'A Mapmaker’s Letter': '地図職人の手紙',
  '“I have walked from the frozen north to the island coasts, and still my map is not finished. Every time I return to a place, a path has moved or a ruin has appeared where there was none. I begin to think the world is still being drawn, and that we are walking on wet ink.”': '「凍てつく北から島々の海岸まで歩いたが、地図はまだ完成しない。同じ場所に戻るたび、道が移動していたり、なかった場所に遺跡が現れたりする。世界は今も描かれていて、私たちは乾かぬ墨の上を歩いているのではないかと思い始めた。」',
  'The Edge of the Page': 'ページの端',
  'Sail far enough in any direction and the sea grows pale, then white. Sailors who came back said the water simply stops being drawn. The Quill scholars believed the edge of the world is not an end but a margin, waiting for someone to write in it.': 'どの方角へも十分遠く航海すると、海は色あせ、やがて白くなる。戻ってきた船乗りたちは、そこで水の描写が途切れると言った。羽根ペン帝国の学者たちは、世界の端は終わりではなく、誰かが書き込むのを待つ余白だと信じていた。',
  'The Dotted Borders': '点線の国境',
  'An old boundary stone. One side reads “Here ends the land of one crown.” The other side reads “Here begins the land of another.” Someone has scratched beneath both: “The grass does not know the difference.”': '古い境界石。片側には「ここから一つの王冠の土地が終わる」、もう片側には「ここから別の王冠の土地が始まる」とある。誰かがその下に刻んでいた。「草はその違いを知らない。」',
  'The Last Page': '最後のページ',
  'The final stone is cracked through the middle. What can still be read says that the First Scribe left one page unwritten, hidden somewhere in the world. Whoever finds it, and holds a pen steady enough, will write how this story ends.': '最後の石碑は真ん中から割れている。かろうじて読める部分には、最初の書記が一枚のページを白紙のまま残し、世界のどこかに隠したとある。それを見つけ、揺るがぬ手でペンを持つ者が、この物語の結末を書く。'
});
addLang('zh', {
  'The Eleven Hands': '十一只手',
  'The Scribe did not draw every people with the same hand. Frost and pine came from one, grass and horses from another, bamboo and mist, monsoon and river, savanna and drum, sand and stars, birch and snowfields, cherry and mountain, island and tide, olive and sea, terrace and condor. That is why no two peoples see the world in the same way, and why each is right.': '书吏并非用同一只手绘出所有民族。霜雪与松林来自一只手，草原与骏马来自另一只；还有竹林与雾气、季风与河流、草原与鼓声、沙漠与星辰、白桦与雪原、樱花与高山、岛屿与潮汐、橄榄与海洋、梯田与神鹰。因此，没有两个民族以相同的方式看待世界，而他们各自都没有错。',
  'The Empire of the Quill': '羽笔帝国',
  'Long before the eleven crowns, one empire spoke for the whole page. Its scholars wrote laws in a script every people could read, and its roads ran from the ice to the desert. These broken walls are what remains of it. The empire called itself the Quill, because it believed it could finish what the Scribe began.': '在十一王冠出现很久以前，曾有一个帝国代表整页世界发声。帝国的学者用所有民族都能读懂的文字书写律法，道路从冰原一直延伸到沙漠。这些断壁残垣就是它仅存的遗迹。帝国自称羽笔，因为它相信自己能完成书吏开创的事业。',
  'The Great Blot': '大墨灾',
  'In the reign of the last Quill Emperor, the scholars tried to write a new land into the world. The ink would not obey. It spilled across the page in a single night, black and wet, and where it dried the land was changed. The chronicles call that night the Great Blot. Most of the chronicles end there.': '最后一位羽笔皇帝在位时，学者们试图将一片新土地写入世界。墨水不听使唤，一夜之间便漆黑潮湿地泼洒整页；墨迹干涸之处，大地随之改变。编年史将那一夜称为“大墨灾”。大多数史书也在此处戛然而止。',
  'Ink Given Fear': '墨魇化形',
  'The Blot has no shape of its own. It takes the shape of what people fear most. In the north it rose as the restless dead, in the islands as the aswang, in the forests as the leshy, in the desert as the ghoul. Each people met the monsters of its own stories. Do not mock another land’s legends. Here, legends bite.': '墨灾本身没有形体，它会化作人们最恐惧的模样。在北方，它化为躁动的亡者；在群岛，化为aswang；在森林，化为leshy；在沙漠，化为ghoul。每个民族都遇上了自己故事中的怪物。别嘲笑别处的传说。在这里，传说会咬人。',
  'The Sealing of the Deep Places': '深渊封印',
  'The strongest creatures of the Blot could not be killed, so the last mages of the Quill sealed them underground: in barrows, caves, tombs and sunken temples, one in every land. The seals were written to last a thousand years. Some say it has been longer than that.': '墨灾中最强大的生物无法杀死，于是羽笔帝国最后的法师将它们封在地下：土冢、洞穴、墓室与沉没的神殿，每片土地各有一处。封印原本应维持千年。有人说，时间早已超过千年。',
  'The Fall of Scriptoria': 'Scriptoria的陷落',
  'The Quill capital, Scriptoria, stood at the centre of the world, where all the lands meet. When the Blot came, the city was drowned in ink. Nothing of it can be seen now, but travellers who stand at the heart of the world say the ground is warmer there, and the grass grows in straight lines, like handwriting.': '羽笔帝国的首都Scriptoria位于世界中心，所有土地在此交会。墨灾降临时，整座城被墨水吞没。如今已看不到任何遗迹，但站在世界中心的旅人说，那里的地面更温暖，青草也像笔迹一样笔直生长。',
  'The Eleven Crowns': '十一王冠',
  'After the fall, the survivors gathered under eleven banners, one for each of the Scribe’s hands. They swore never to let a single empire rule the page again. They have kept that oath, mostly by fighting one another. Alliances are signed and torn, and borders are drawn in dotted lines because no one trusts them to stay.': '帝国覆灭后，幸存者聚集在十一面旗帜之下，每面旗帜都象征书吏的一只手。他们发誓不让任何单一帝国再度统治整页世界。他们确实守住了誓言——主要靠彼此征战。盟约签了又撕，边界则画成虚线，因为没人相信它们能长久。',
  'The Hunters’ Oath': '猎人誓言',
  'Carved above a doorway: “We hunt what crawls out of the Blot, in every land and under every banner. We take no side in the quarrels of crowns. A monster slain in an enemy’s field is still a monster slain.” This was the first hall of the Hunters’ Lodge.': '门楣上刻着：“无论身处何地、属于何种旗帜，我们都猎杀从墨灾中爬出的东西。王冠之间的纷争，我们不站队。在敌人的土地上杀死怪物，依旧是杀死怪物。”这里曾是猎人公馆的第一座大厅。',
  'Ink in the Blood': '血脉中的墨',
  'When a great creature of the Blot is destroyed, its ink does not vanish. Some of it passes into the one who struck it down. That is where the old powers come from: frost, fire, the roar, the stomp of a titan. The Quill forbade such power. The Quill is gone.': '墨灾中的强大生物被消灭后，墨迹并不会消失。一部分会流入击败它的人体内。古老的力量由此而来：寒霜、烈火、咆哮、泰坦的践踏。羽笔帝国曾禁止这种力量。如今帝国已不复存在。',
  'A Mapmaker’s Letter': '制图师的信',
  '“I have walked from the frozen north to the island coasts, and still my map is not finished. Every time I return to a place, a path has moved or a ruin has appeared where there was none. I begin to think the world is still being drawn, and that we are walking on wet ink.”': '“我从冰封的北方走到群岛海岸，地图却仍未完成。每次回到某处，总有道路移了位置，或是凭空多出一座遗迹。我渐渐觉得，世界仍在被描绘，而我们正走在未干的墨迹上。”',
  'The Edge of the Page': '页边',
  'Sail far enough in any direction and the sea grows pale, then white. Sailors who came back said the water simply stops being drawn. The Quill scholars believed the edge of the world is not an end but a margin, waiting for someone to write in it.': '朝任意方向航行得足够远，海面便会逐渐褪色，最终变成一片白。归来的水手说，海水在那里仿佛不再被描绘。羽笔帝国的学者认为，世界的边缘并非终点，而是一片等待人书写的页边空白。',
  'The Dotted Borders': '虚线边界',
  'An old boundary stone. One side reads “Here ends the land of one crown.” The other side reads “Here begins the land of another.” Someone has scratched beneath both: “The grass does not know the difference.”': '一块古老的界碑。一面写着：“一顶王冠的领土到此为止。”另一面写着：“另一顶王冠的领土从此开始。”有人在两行字下刻道：“青草分不清其中的区别。”',
  'The Last Page': '最后一页',
  'The final stone is cracked through the middle. What can still be read says that the First Scribe left one page unwritten, hidden somewhere in the world. Whoever finds it, and holds a pen steady enough, will write how this story ends.': '最后一块石碑从中裂开。仍可辨认的文字说，最初的书吏留下一页未写的空白，将它藏在世界某处。找到它并能稳稳执笔的人，将写下这个故事的结局。'
});

