addLang('ja', {
  'Long before the novels, villagers in Serbia and the Balkans told of the vampir: a dead man who rose from his grave at night to drink the blood of the living. In 1725 a Serbian villager named Petar Blagojević was said to have come back after death and strangled nine neighbours, and Austrian officials dug up his body. They found it fresh, with new skin and nails, and blood around the mouth. The report spread across Europe and gave the world the word ‘vampire’.': '小説が書かれるよりはるか昔から、セルビアやバルカンの村人はヴァンピルの話をしていた。夜に墓から起き上がり、生者の血を飲む死者だ。1725年、セルビアの村人ペタル・ブラゴイェヴィッチが死後に蘇り、近隣住民九人を絞め殺したと噂された。オーストリアの役人が遺体を掘り起こすと、肌も爪も新しく、口の周りに血がついていたという。この報告はヨーロッパ中に広まり、「ヴァンパイア」という言葉を世に知らしめた。',
  'Slavic villagers drove a stake of hawthorn or ash through the heart, put garlic and a sickle in the grave, or scattered poppy seeds on the road: a vampire must stop and count every seed until sunrise.': 'スラヴの村人はサンザシやトネリコの杭を心臓に打ち込み、墓にニンニクと鎌を入れたり、道にケシの実をまいたりした。吸血鬼は夜明けまで一粒ずつ数えなければならず、足止めできるのだ。',
  'Across Europe, people believed some men could turn into wolves. In Slavic lands they were called vlkodlak or volkolak, ‘wolf-hair’. Some were born with a caul or a wolf’s birthmark; others were cursed by a witch, or put on a wolf’s belt to change shape. The old Russian epic tells of Prince Vseslav of Polotsk, a sorcerer who ran as a wolf at night from Kiev to the sea and back before the roosters crowed.': 'ヨーロッパ各地では、狼に変身する男がいると信じられていた。スラヴの地では「狼の毛」を意味するvlkodlak、またはvolkolakと呼ばれた。羊膜をかぶって生まれたり、狼の形のあざがあったりする者もいれば、魔女に呪われたり、狼の帯を身につけて変身したりする者もいた。古いロシア叙事詩には、魔術師であるポロツク公フセスラフの物語がある。夜、狼の姿でキエフから海まで走り、鶏が鳴く前に戻ってきたという。',
  'A werewolf turns back into a man if you call him by his Christian name, or if you wound him and draw blood. In later tales, only silver can kill one.': '人狼は、洗礼名で呼ばれるか、傷を負って血を流すと人間に戻る。後世の物語では、銀だけが人狼を殺せる。',
  'The leshy is the master of the forest. He can be as tall as the tallest tree or as small as a blade of grass, with a beard of moss and eyes that glow green. He leads travellers in circles until they are lost, imitates the voices of friends calling for help, and laughs and whistles in the dark. Hunters and herdsmen made deals with him, leaving bread and salt on a stump so he would let them come home safely.': 'レーシーは森の主だ。最も高い木ほど大きくも、草の葉ほど小さくもなり、苔のひげと緑に光る目を持つ。旅人を迷うまで輪の中に導き、助けを呼ぶ友人の声をまね、暗闇で笑い口笛を吹く。狩人や牧夫は彼と取引し、切り株にパンと塩を置いて、無事に帰れるよう頼んだ。',
  'If the leshy has made you lost, take off your clothes and put them back on inside out, and put your left shoe on your right foot. The trick confuses him and the path appears again.': 'レーシーに道を迷わされたら、服を脱いで裏返しに着直し、左の靴を右足に履こう。この策で彼を混乱させると、再び道が現れる。'
});
addLang('zh', {
  'Long before the novels, villagers in Serbia and the Balkans told of the vampir: a dead man who rose from his grave at night to drink the blood of the living. In 1725 a Serbian villager named Petar Blagojević was said to have come back after death and strangled nine neighbours, and Austrian officials dug up his body. They found it fresh, with new skin and nails, and blood around the mouth. The report spread across Europe and gave the world the word ‘vampire’.': '早在吸血鬼小说出现之前，塞尔维亚和巴尔干地区的村民就讲述过“vampir”的传说：一名死者夜里从坟墓中爬出，吸食活人的血。1725年，据说塞尔维亚村民佩塔尔·布拉戈耶维奇死后归来，勒死了九名邻居。奥地利官员掘开他的尸体，发现尸身鲜活，长出了新皮肤和指甲，嘴边还有血迹。这份报告传遍欧洲，“vampire”一词也由此闻名。',
  'Slavic villagers drove a stake of hawthorn or ash through the heart, put garlic and a sickle in the grave, or scattered poppy seeds on the road: a vampire must stop and count every seed until sunrise.': '斯拉夫村民会把山楂木或白蜡木桩刺入心脏，在墓中放入大蒜和镰刀，或在路上撒下罂粟籽：吸血鬼必须停下来数完每一粒，直到日出。',
  'Across Europe, people believed some men could turn into wolves. In Slavic lands they were called vlkodlak or volkolak, ‘wolf-hair’. Some were born with a caul or a wolf’s birthmark; others were cursed by a witch, or put on a wolf’s belt to change shape. The old Russian epic tells of Prince Vseslav of Polotsk, a sorcerer who ran as a wolf at night from Kiev to the sea and back before the roosters crowed.': '欧洲各地都有人相信，某些男子能够变成狼。在斯拉夫地区，他们被称为vlkodlak或volkolak，意为“狼毛”。有些人出生时带着胎膜或狼形胎记；另一些人遭女巫诅咒，或系上狼皮腰带变身。古老的俄罗斯史诗讲述了波洛茨克公爵弗谢斯拉夫的故事：他是位术士，夜里化作狼，从基辅奔到海边，再赶在鸡鸣前返回。',
  'A werewolf turns back into a man if you call him by his Christian name, or if you wound him and draw blood. In later tales, only silver can kill one.': '只要用他的受洗名呼唤他，或刺伤他使其流血，狼人就会变回人形。后来的传说则说，只有银器才能杀死狼人。',
  'The leshy is the master of the forest. He can be as tall as the tallest tree or as small as a blade of grass, with a beard of moss and eyes that glow green. He leads travellers in circles until they are lost, imitates the voices of friends calling for help, and laughs and whistles in the dark. Hunters and herdsmen made deals with him, leaving bread and salt on a stump so he would let them come home safely.': '列希是森林的主宰。他可以高如最高的树，也可以小如一片草叶；长着苔藓胡须，双眼泛着绿光。他会引着旅人兜圈，直到他们迷路；模仿朋友呼救的声音，在黑暗中大笑、吹口哨。猎人和牧人会与他做交易，在树桩上留下面包和盐，求他放自己平安回家。',
  'If the leshy has made you lost, take off your clothes and put them back on inside out, and put your left shoe on your right foot. The trick confuses him and the path appears again.': '如果列希让你迷了路，就脱下衣服再反着穿上，并把左脚的鞋穿到右脚上。这样能迷惑他，让道路重新显现。'
});

// Book of Monsters: Volkmark entries, second batch.
addLang('fr', {
  'A rusalka is the spirit of a young woman who drowned, often a girl betrayed in love. She lives in the river and comes out in early summer, during Rusalka Week, to sit on birch branches and comb her long green hair. She sings and dances in the moonlight and calls young men to dance with her. Those who join her are tickled or danced to death, or pulled down into the water.': 'Une rusalka est l’esprit d’une jeune femme noyée, souvent une fille trahie en amour. Elle vit dans la rivière et en sort au début de l’été, pendant la Semaine des Rusalki, pour s’asseoir sur les branches de bouleau et peigner ses longs cheveux verts. Au clair de lune, elle chante, danse et invite les jeunes hommes à la rejoindre. Ceux qui acceptent sont chatouillés ou entraînés dans une danse mortelle, puis emportés sous l’eau.',
  'Carry wormwood: if a rusalka asks what herb you hold, answer ‘wormwood’ and she will flee. During Rusalka Week, villagers did not swim and hung embroidered cloths in the birch trees as gifts.': 'Porte de l’armoise : si une rusalka te demande quelle herbe tu tiens, réponds « armoise » et elle s’enfuira. Pendant la Semaine des Rusalki, les villageois évitaient de nager et accrochaient des étoffes brodées aux bouleaux en offrande.',
  'Zmey Gorynych is the great dragon of Russian legend, with three heads (sometimes more), who breathes fire and lives in the mountains. He flies over villages to steal young women and princesses. In the bylina of Dobrynya Nikitich, the hero first beats the dragon while bathing in the Puchai river, foolishly spares him, and must fight him again three days long to free the prince’s niece Zabava.': 'Zmey Gorynych est le grand dragon des légendes russes. Il a trois têtes, parfois davantage, crache du feu et vit dans les montagnes. Il survole les villages pour enlever des jeunes femmes et des princesses. Dans la bylina de Dobrynya Nikitich, le héros le vainc d’abord alors qu’il se baigne dans la rivière Poutchaï, mais l’épargne par folie ; il doit alors le combattre de nouveau pendant trois jours pour libérer Zabava, la nièce du prince.',
  'When Dobrynya fought him, the dragon’s blood would not soak into the ground and nearly drowned the hero, until he begged Mother Earth to drink it. Cutting off a head is not enough: some heads grow back unless burned.': 'Lors du combat contre Dobrynya, le sang du dragon ne pénétrait pas dans le sol et faillit noyer le héros, jusqu’à ce qu’il supplie la Terre-Mère de le boire. Lui couper une tête ne suffit pas : certaines repoussent si on ne les brûle pas.',
  'The kikimora is a small, thin house spirit with a tiny head the size of a thimble and a body as thin as straw. She lives behind the stove or in the cellar. If the house is kept well, she helps with the chores and spins at night. But if the family is lazy, she breaks dishes, tangles the yarn, plucks the chickens and whistles in the dark. Some say if you see her spinning, someone in the house will soon die.': 'La kikimora est un petit esprit domestique maigre, avec une tête minuscule comme un dé à coudre et un corps fin comme de la paille. Elle vit derrière le poêle ou dans la cave. Si la maison est bien tenue, elle aide aux tâches ménagères et file la nuit. Mais si la famille est paresseuse, elle casse la vaisselle, emmêle le fil, plume les poules et siffle dans le noir. Selon certains, voir la kikimora filer annonce la mort prochaine d’un membre du foyer.',
  'A tea of ferns used to wash the pots and dishes chases her away. Keep the house clean and tidy and she will leave you in peace.': 'Une décoction de fougères utilisée pour laver les casseroles et la vaisselle la chasse. Garde la maison propre et rangée, et elle te laissera en paix.'
});
addLang('es', {
  'A rusalka is the spirit of a young woman who drowned, often a girl betrayed in love. She lives in the river and comes out in early summer, during Rusalka Week, to sit on birch branches and comb her long green hair. She sings and dances in the moonlight and calls young men to dance with her. Those who join her are tickled or danced to death, or pulled down into the water.': 'Una rusalka es el espíritu de una joven ahogada, a menudo una muchacha traicionada en el amor. Vive en el río y sale al comienzo del verano, durante la Semana de las Rusalkas, para sentarse en las ramas de los abedules y peinarse el largo cabello verde. Canta y baila a la luz de la luna e invita a los jóvenes a bailar con ella. A quienes aceptan los hace morir de cosquillas o de tanto bailar, o los arrastra bajo el agua.',
  'Carry wormwood: if a rusalka asks what herb you hold, answer ‘wormwood’ and she will flee. During Rusalka Week, villagers did not swim and hung embroidered cloths in the birch trees as gifts.': 'Lleva ajenjo: si una rusalka te pregunta qué hierba tienes, responde «ajenjo» y huirá. Durante la Semana de las Rusalkas, los aldeanos no se bañaban y colgaban telas bordadas en los abedules como ofrenda.',
  'Zmey Gorynych is the great dragon of Russian legend, with three heads (sometimes more), who breathes fire and lives in the mountains. He flies over villages to steal young women and princesses. In the bylina of Dobrynya Nikitich, the hero first beats the dragon while bathing in the Puchai river, foolishly spares him, and must fight him again three days long to free the prince’s niece Zabava.': 'Zmey Gorynych es el gran dragón de las leyendas rusas: tiene tres cabezas, a veces más, escupe fuego y vive en las montañas. Sobrevuela aldeas para raptar a jóvenes y princesas. En la bylina de Dobrynya Nikitich, el héroe lo vence primero mientras se baña en el río Puchai, pero comete la imprudencia de perdonarlo. Luego debe combatirlo durante tres días para liberar a Zabava, la sobrina del príncipe.',
  'When Dobrynya fought him, the dragon’s blood would not soak into the ground and nearly drowned the hero, until he begged Mother Earth to drink it. Cutting off a head is not enough: some heads grow back unless burned.': 'Cuando Dobrynya luchó contra él, la sangre del dragón no se filtraba en la tierra y casi ahogó al héroe, hasta que este suplicó a la Madre Tierra que la absorbiera. Cortarle una cabeza no basta: algunas vuelven a crecer si no se queman.',
  'The kikimora is a small, thin house spirit with a tiny head the size of a thimble and a body as thin as straw. She lives behind the stove or in the cellar. If the house is kept well, she helps with the chores and spins at night. But if the family is lazy, she breaks dishes, tangles the yarn, plucks the chickens and whistles in the dark. Some say if you see her spinning, someone in the house will soon die.': 'La kikimora es un espíritu doméstico pequeño y delgado, con una cabeza diminuta como un dedal y un cuerpo fino como la paja. Vive detrás de la estufa o en el sótano. Si la casa está bien cuidada, ayuda con las tareas y hila por la noche. Pero si la familia es perezosa, rompe la vajilla, enreda el hilo, despluma las gallinas y silba en la oscuridad. Algunos dicen que verla hilar anuncia que alguien de la casa morirá pronto.',
  'A tea of ferns used to wash the pots and dishes chases her away. Keep the house clean and tidy and she will leave you in peace.': 'Una infusión de helechos para lavar las ollas y los platos la ahuyenta. Mantén la casa limpia y ordenada y te dejará en paz.'
});
addLang('pt', {
  'A rusalka is the spirit of a young woman who drowned, often a girl betrayed in love. She lives in the river and comes out in early summer, during Rusalka Week, to sit on birch branches and comb her long green hair. She sings and dances in the moonlight and calls young men to dance with her. Those who join her are tickled or danced to death, or pulled down into the water.': 'Uma rusalka é o espírito de uma jovem que se afogou, muitas vezes uma rapariga traída no amor. Vive no rio e, no início do verão, durante a Semana das Rusalkas, sai para se sentar nos ramos das bétulas e pentear os longos cabelos verdes. Canta e dança ao luar, chamando os rapazes para dançarem com ela. Quem aceita morre de cócegas ou de tanto dançar, ou é puxado para debaixo de água.',
  'Carry wormwood: if a rusalka asks what herb you hold, answer ‘wormwood’ and she will flee. During Rusalka Week, villagers did not swim and hung embroidered cloths in the birch trees as gifts.': 'Leva losna contigo: se uma rusalka perguntar que erva tens, responde «losna» e ela fugirá. Durante a Semana das Rusalkas, os aldeões não nadavam e penduravam tecidos bordados nas bétulas como oferenda.',
  'Zmey Gorynych is the great dragon of Russian legend, with three heads (sometimes more), who breathes fire and lives in the mountains. He flies over villages to steal young women and princesses. In the bylina of Dobrynya Nikitich, the hero first beats the dragon while bathing in the Puchai river, foolishly spares him, and must fight him again three days long to free the prince’s niece Zabava.': 'Zmey Gorynych é o grande dragão das lendas russas: tem três cabeças, por vezes mais, cospe fogo e vive nas montanhas. Voa sobre as aldeias para raptar jovens e princesas. Na bylina de Dobrynya Nikitich, o herói vence-o primeiro enquanto toma banho no rio Puchai, mas, imprudentemente, poupa-lhe a vida. Tem de voltar a combatê-lo durante três dias para libertar Zabava, sobrinha do príncipe.',
  'When Dobrynya fought him, the dragon’s blood would not soak into the ground and nearly drowned the hero, until he begged Mother Earth to drink it. Cutting off a head is not enough: some heads grow back unless burned.': 'Quando Dobrynya lutou contra ele, o sangue do dragão não penetrava no chão e quase afogou o herói, até este implorar à Mãe Terra que o bebesse. Cortar uma cabeça não chega: algumas voltam a crescer se não forem queimadas.',
  'The kikimora is a small, thin house spirit with a tiny head the size of a thimble and a body as thin as straw. She lives behind the stove or in the cellar. If the house is kept well, she helps with the chores and spins at night. But if the family is lazy, she breaks dishes, tangles the yarn, plucks the chickens and whistles in the dark. Some say if you see her spinning, someone in the house will soon die.': 'A kikimora é um pequeno e magro espírito doméstico, com uma cabeça minúscula do tamanho de um dedal e o corpo fino como palha. Vive atrás do fogão ou na cave. Se a casa for bem cuidada, ajuda nas tarefas e fia à noite. Mas, se a família for preguiçosa, parte a loiça, emaranha o fio, depena as galinhas e assobia no escuro. Alguns dizem que vê-la a fiar anuncia a morte próxima de alguém da casa.',
  'A tea of ferns used to wash the pots and dishes chases her away. Keep the house clean and tidy and she will leave you in peace.': 'Uma infusão de fetos usada para lavar tachos e pratos afasta-a. Mantém a casa limpa e arrumada e ela deixará de te incomodar.'
});
addLang('ja', {
  'A rusalka is the spirit of a young woman who drowned, often a girl betrayed in love. She lives in the river and comes out in early summer, during Rusalka Week, to sit on birch branches and comb her long green hair. She sings and dances in the moonlight and calls young men to dance with her. Those who join her are tickled or danced to death, or pulled down into the water.': 'ルサールカは、溺れて死んだ若い女性の霊だ。恋に裏切られた娘であることが多い。川に住み、初夏のルサールカ週間になると岸辺に現れ、白樺の枝に座って長い緑の髪をとかす。月明かりの下で歌い踊り、若者を誘う。誘いに乗った者はくすぐられたり、踊り死ぬまで踊らされたり、水中へ引きずり込まれたりする。',
  'Carry wormwood: if a rusalka asks what herb you hold, answer ‘wormwood’ and she will flee. During Rusalka Week, villagers did not swim and hung embroidered cloths in the birch trees as gifts.': 'ニガヨモギを持ち歩こう。ルサールカに何の草か尋ねられたら「ニガヨモギ」と答えれば、彼女は逃げる。ルサールカ週間には村人は泳がず、贈り物として刺繍布を白樺に吊るした。',
  'Zmey Gorynych is the great dragon of Russian legend, with three heads (sometimes more), who breathes fire and lives in the mountains. He flies over villages to steal young women and princesses. In the bylina of Dobrynya Nikitich, the hero first beats the dragon while bathing in the Puchai river, foolishly spares him, and must fight him again three days long to free the prince’s niece Zabava.': 'ズメイ・ゴルィニチはロシア伝説の大竜だ。三つ、時にはそれ以上の頭を持ち、火を吐いて山に住む。村の上を飛び、娘や王女をさらう。英雄ドブルィニャ・ニキーティチのブィリーナでは、主人公はプチャイ川で水浴び中に竜を倒すが、愚かにも命を助けてしまう。そのため王子の姪ザバーヴァを救うべく、さらに三日間戦うことになる。',
  'When Dobrynya fought him, the dragon’s blood would not soak into the ground and nearly drowned the hero, until he begged Mother Earth to drink it. Cutting off a head is not enough: some heads grow back unless burned.': 'ドブルィニャが戦ったとき、竜の血は地面に染み込まず、英雄を溺れさせかけた。彼が母なる大地に血を飲んでくれと願うまで、血はあふれ続けた。頭を切り落とすだけでは足りない。焼かなければ再生する頭もある。',
  'The kikimora is a small, thin house spirit with a tiny head the size of a thimble and a body as thin as straw. She lives behind the stove or in the cellar. If the house is kept well, she helps with the chores and spins at night. But if the family is lazy, she breaks dishes, tangles the yarn, plucks the chickens and whistles in the dark. Some say if you see her spinning, someone in the house will soon die.': 'キキーモラは小柄で細い家の精霊だ。頭は指ぬきほど小さく、体は藁のように細い。かまどの裏や地下室に住み、家がきちんと整っていれば家事を手伝い、夜には糸を紡ぐ。しかし家族が怠けると、皿を割り、糸を絡ませ、鶏の羽をむしり、暗闇で口笛を吹く。彼女が糸を紡ぐ姿を見ると、その家の誰かが間もなく死ぬという。',
  'A tea of ferns used to wash the pots and dishes chases her away. Keep the house clean and tidy and she will leave you in peace.': '鍋や皿を洗うときにシダの煎じ液を使えば、彼女を追い払える。家を清潔に整えておけば、そっとしておいてくれる。'
});
addLang('zh', {
  'A rusalka is the spirit of a young woman who drowned, often a girl betrayed in love. She lives in the river and comes out in early summer, during Rusalka Week, to sit on birch branches and comb her long green hair. She sings and dances in the moonlight and calls young men to dance with her. Those who join her are tickled or danced to death, or pulled down into the water.': '鲁萨尔卡是溺亡的年轻女子之灵，常常是被爱情背叛的少女。她居于河中，初夏的鲁萨尔卡周会浮出水面，坐在白桦枝上梳理长长的绿发。她在月光下歌舞，邀请年轻男子共舞。接受邀请的人会被搔痒至死、被迫跳舞至死，或被拖入水底。',
  'Carry wormwood: if a rusalka asks what herb you hold, answer ‘wormwood’ and she will flee. During Rusalka Week, villagers did not swim and hung embroidered cloths in the birch trees as gifts.': '随身带着苦艾：鲁萨尔卡若问你拿的是什么草，就回答“苦艾”，她便会逃走。鲁萨尔卡周期间，村民不下水游泳，还会把绣花布挂在白桦树上作为礼物。',
  'Zmey Gorynych is the great dragon of Russian legend, with three heads (sometimes more), who breathes fire and lives in the mountains. He flies over villages to steal young women and princesses. In the bylina of Dobrynya Nikitich, the hero first beats the dragon while bathing in the Puchai river, foolishly spares him, and must fight him again three days long to free the prince’s niece Zabava.': '兹梅·戈雷尼奇是俄罗斯传说中的巨龙，长着三颗头（有时更多），会喷火，居住在山中。他飞过村庄，掳走年轻女子和公主。在英雄多布雷尼亚·尼基季奇的史诗中，英雄在普恰伊河沐浴时首次击败巨龙，却愚蠢地放过了它。为了救出王子的侄女扎巴瓦，他不得不再与巨龙鏖战三天。',
  'When Dobrynya fought him, the dragon’s blood would not soak into the ground and nearly drowned the hero, until he begged Mother Earth to drink it. Cutting off a head is not enough: some heads grow back unless burned.': '多布雷尼亚与巨龙交战时，龙血无法渗入土地，险些将英雄淹没，直到他恳求大地母亲把血吸走。光砍掉一颗头还不够：有些头若不烧掉，还会重新长出。',
  'The kikimora is a small, thin house spirit with a tiny head the size of a thimble and a body as thin as straw. She lives behind the stove or in the cellar. If the house is kept well, she helps with the chores and spins at night. But if the family is lazy, she breaks dishes, tangles the yarn, plucks the chickens and whistles in the dark. Some say if you see her spinning, someone in the house will soon die.': '基基莫拉是一种矮小纤瘦的家宅精灵，脑袋只有顶针那么大，身子细如稻草。她住在炉灶后或地窖里。若家中整洁，她会帮忙做家务，夜里纺线；若一家人懒惰，她便打碎碗碟、搅乱毛线、拔光鸡毛，还在黑暗中吹口哨。有人说，看见她纺线，家里很快就会有人死去。',
  'A tea of ferns used to wash the pots and dishes chases her away. Keep the house clean and tidy and she will leave you in peace.': '用蕨类煮成的茶水洗锅碗可以赶走她。把家里收拾干净整齐，她就会让你安宁。'
});

