addLang('zh', {
'The qutrub is a creature of the graveyards and the night, like a werewolf or a male ghoul. It wanders tombs, eats the dead and attacks lonely travellers. Arab writers called it the male of the si’lat, and doctors in the Middle Ages even gave its name, ‘qutrub’, to a kind of madness where people wander tombs at night, howling.':'库特鲁布是墓地与夜晚的怪物，类似狼人或雄性食尸鬼。它游荡坟墓、吞食尸体、袭击孤身旅人。阿拉伯作者称它为西拉特的雄性；中世纪医生甚至用它的名字“库特鲁布”指一种夜里在墓地游荡嚎叫的疯症。',
'Qutrubs avoid lights and crowds. Travel by day, with others, and never sleep in a graveyard.':'库特鲁布躲避灯火和人群。白天结伴旅行，千万不要在墓地过夜。',
'The ifrit is a powerful and dangerous kind of jinn made of smokeless fire, living in ruins and the underground. In the Qur’an, an ifrit offers to bring King Solomon the throne of the Queen of Sheba before Solomon can rise from his seat. In the Thousand and One Nights, ifrits kidnap princesses on their wedding nights, imprison them in glass chests and turn men into apes.':'伊夫利特是强大而危险的一类精灵，由无烟之火构成，栖身废墟和地下。《古兰经》中，一只伊夫利特表示可在所罗门王起身前，将示巴女王的宝座搬来。《一千零一夜》中，伊夫利特会在公主新婚之夜掳走她们、囚于玻璃箱中，还会把男子变成猿猴。',
'An ifrit can be bound with Solomon’s seal or by a powerful sorcerer, and then must obey. Like all jinn, it is said to fear iron and the words of holy verses.':'所罗门印记或强大的术士可以束缚伊夫利特，令其服从。据说它和所有精灵一样，害怕铁器与圣典经文。',
'The great roc was so vast that its wings hid the sun. Marco Polo wrote in the 1290s that the people of Madagascar told of a giant bird that lifted elephants into the sky and dropped them to break them open. The Great Khan himself was said to have received a roc feather twelve paces long. Sinbad saw one rest on an island, and thought the sun had gone out.':'大鲁克鸟巨大无比，双翼能遮蔽太阳。马可·波罗在1290年代记载，马达加斯加人传说有巨鸟把大象抓上天空再摔下，使其裂开。据说大汗曾收到一根长达十二步的鲁克鸟羽毛。辛巴达看见它落在岛上，还以为太阳熄灭了。',
'The great roc is too big to dodge in the air: it strikes like a falling mountain. Hide in caves and gorges, where its wings cannot follow.':'大鲁克鸟体形庞大，在空中无法躲避，撞来如山崩。躲进它翅膀无法进入的洞穴或峡谷。',
'The Minotaur had the body of a man and the head of a bull. It was the son of Queen Pasiphaë of Crete and a white bull sent by the sea god Poseidon. King Minos had the inventor Daedalus build the Labyrinth to imprison it, and every nine years Athens had to send seven youths and seven maidens to be eaten. The hero Theseus went in, killed the Minotaur, and found his way out with a ball of thread given by Princess Ariadne.':'弥诺陶洛斯长着人的身体和公牛的头，是克里特王后帕西淮与海神波塞冬送来的白牛所生。弥诺斯王让发明家代达罗斯建造迷宫囚禁它；雅典每九年须送来七名少年和七名少女供它吞食。英雄忒修斯进入迷宫杀死它，凭阿里阿德涅公主赠予的线团找到出口。',
'Take a thread into the maze and unroll it as you go, like Theseus. The Minotaur knows every corner of its Labyrinth, but the thread will lead you out.':'像忒修斯一样，带一团线进迷宫，边走边放线。弥诺陶洛斯熟悉迷宫的每个角落，但线会指引你出去。',
'Harpies are winged women with the claws of birds of prey, called ‘the snatchers’. They were sent by Zeus to punish King Phineus of Thrace: every time he sat down to eat, the harpies swooped down to steal his food and foul the rest. When Jason and the Argonauts passed by, the winged brothers Zetes and Calais, sons of the North Wind, chased the harpies across the sea until the goddess Iris stopped them.':'哈耳庇厄是长着猛禽利爪的飞翼女子，又称“掠夺者”。宙斯派她们惩罚色雷斯王菲纽斯：他每次用餐，她们便俯冲下来抢走食物、弄脏剩下的部分。伊阿宋和阿尔戈英雄路过时，北风之子、长翅膀的兄弟泽忒斯和卡莱斯一路追赶她们越过海面，直到女神伊里斯出面阻止。',
'The sons of the North Wind beat the harpies with speed. Harpies avoid places protected by the gods, and their shrieks can be drowned out by bronze cymbals, as Heracles scared off the Stymphalian birds.':'北风之子凭速度战胜了哈耳庇厄。她们会避开受神明庇护之地；铜钹的响声也能盖过她们的尖叫，正如赫拉克勒斯曾用响声驱赶斯廷法罗斯湖怪鸟。',
'The Gorgons were three sisters with snakes for hair, golden wings and brass hands. One look at their faces turned anyone to stone. Two were immortal; only Medusa could die. The hero Perseus, helped by Athena and Hermes, flew to the edge of the world with winged sandals, looked only at Medusa’s reflection in his polished bronze shield, and cut off her head. From her blood sprang the winged horse Pegasus.':'戈耳工三姐妹长着蛇发、金色翅膀和青铜手。只要看她们一眼，人就会化为石头。两位不死，只有美杜莎会死。在雅典娜与赫尔墨斯帮助下，英雄珀尔修斯穿上飞鞋来到世界尽头，只看美杜莎映在抛光青铜盾上的倒影，随后斩下她的头。飞马珀伽索斯从她的血中诞生。',
'Never look a gorgon in the face: watch her only in a mirror or a polished shield. Even after death, a gorgon’s head can still turn enemies to stone.':'不要直视戈耳工：只能通过镜子或磨光的盾牌观察。即使死后，戈耳工的头仍能把敌人变成石头。',
'Satyrs are wild men of the woods with horse or goat ears, tails and sometimes goat legs, companions of Dionysus, the god of wine. They love music, dancing, wine and chasing nymphs, and they are lazy, greedy and cowardly. The satyr Marsyas found Athena’s flute and dared to challenge the god Apollo to a music contest; he lost, and Apollo flayed him alive for his pride.':'萨提尔是森林中的野人，长有马或山羊的耳朵、尾巴，有时还有山羊腿，是酒神狄俄倪索斯的伙伴。他们爱音乐、舞蹈、美酒和追逐宁芙，懒惰、贪婪又胆怯。萨提尔马西亚斯找到雅典娜的笛子，竟敢挑战阿波罗比试音乐；他输了，阿波罗因其傲慢将他活活剥皮。',
'Satyrs cannot resist wine and music. King Midas caught the wise old satyr Silenus by pouring wine into the spring he drank from.':'萨提尔无法抗拒美酒和音乐。弥达斯王把酒倒进老智者西勒诺斯常饮的泉水中，将他捉住。',
'Lamia was a beautiful queen of Libya loved by Zeus. In her jealousy, the goddess Hera killed her children. Mad with grief, Lamia became a monster who stole and ate the children of others, and her face became terrible to see. Hera cursed her so she could never close her eyes, but Zeus gave her the power to take her eyes out to rest. Greek mothers used her name to frighten naughty children.':'拉弥亚是宙斯所爱的利比亚美女王后。嫉妒的赫拉杀死了她的孩子。悲痛欲狂的拉弥亚化为怪物，掳走并吞食别人的孩子，面容也变得可怖。赫拉诅咒她永远无法闭眼，但宙斯赐她取出双眼休息的能力。希腊母亲常用她的名字吓唬淘气孩子。',
'Lamia sleeps only when she takes her eyes out of her head. In later tales she charms young men; the philosopher Apollonius saw through one at a wedding feast, and her illusion of gold and servants vanished.':'拉弥亚只有取出眼睛才能入睡。后来的故事里她会迷惑年轻男子；哲学家阿波罗尼奥斯在婚宴上识破了一只拉弥亚，金银与仆人的幻象随即消失。'
});
addLang('fr', {
'The Sirens were half-bird, half-woman creatures who lived on a rocky island surrounded by the bones of sailors. Their song was so beautiful that anyone who heard it forgot home and steered their ship onto the rocks. Odysseus wanted to hear it and live: he plugged his sailors’ ears with beeswax and had himself tied to the mast, ordering them not to untie him, whatever he said.':'Les Sirènes étaient des créatures mi-femmes, mi-oiseaux, qui vivaient sur une île rocheuse entourée des ossements de marins. Leur chant était si beau que ceux qui l’entendaient oubliaient leur foyer et dirigeaient leur navire vers les rochers. Ulysse voulut l’entendre sans mourir : il boucha les oreilles de ses marins avec de la cire et se fit attacher au mât, leur ordonnant de ne jamais le détacher, quoi qu’il dise.',
'Wax in the ears, or a stronger song. When the Argonauts sailed past, Orpheus played his lyre so beautifully that the sailors did not hear the Sirens at all.':'De la cire dans les oreilles, ou un chant plus puissant. Lorsque les Argonautes passèrent, Orphée joua si magnifiquement de sa lyre que les marins n’entendirent pas les Sirènes.',
'The Cyclopes are one-eyed giants. In Homer’s Odyssey, Odysseus and his men are trapped in the cave of Polyphemus, son of Poseidon, who eats two of them every meal. Odysseus tells him his name is ‘Nobody’, makes him drunk with strong wine, and blinds his single eye with a burning stake. When the other Cyclopes ask who hurt him, Polyphemus shouts ‘Nobody is killing me!’ and no one comes to help.':'Les Cyclopes sont des géants à un œil. Dans l’Odyssée d’Homère, Ulysse et ses hommes sont prisonniers de la grotte de Polyphème, fils de Poséidon, qui en dévore deux à chaque repas. Ulysse prétend s’appeler « Personne », enivre le géant avec du vin fort et lui brûle son œil unique avec un pieu. Lorsque les autres Cyclopes lui demandent qui l’a blessé, Polyphème crie : « Personne me tue ! » Personne ne vient l’aider.',
'Strong wine puts a Cyclops to sleep, and his single eye is his weak point. Escape his cave clinging under his sheep: he counts them with his hands, but only feels their backs.':'Le vin fort endort un Cyclope, dont l’œil unique est le point faible. Pour fuir sa grotte, accroche-toi sous ses moutons : il les compte avec ses mains, mais ne sent que leur dos.',
'The Lernaean Hydra was a giant water serpent with many heads, whose breath and blood were deadly poison. Killing it was the second of the twelve labours of Heracles. Each time he cut off a head, two more grew back. His nephew Iolaus helped by burning each neck with a torch so no new heads could grow. The last head was immortal, and Heracles buried it under a huge rock.':'L’Hydre de Lerne était un immense serpent d’eau à plusieurs têtes, dont le souffle et le sang étaient mortellement toxiques. La tuer fut le deuxième des douze travaux d’Héraclès. Chaque tête tranchée repoussait en double. Son neveu Iolaos l’aida en cautérisant chaque cou avec une torche, empêchant les têtes de renaître. La dernière était immortelle ; Héraclès l’enterra sous un énorme rocher.',
'Fire stops the heads from growing back: burn each wound as soon as you cut. Heracles dipped his arrows in the Hydra’s poison, which made them deadly ever after.':'Le feu empêche les têtes de repousser : cautérise chaque plaie dès que tu tranches. Héraclès trempa ses flèches dans le poison de l’Hydre, les rendant mortelles à jamais.',
'The cadejo is a great dog with glowing red eyes and hooves like a goat, that appears to people walking alone at night. There are two: the white cadejo, a guardian sent to protect travellers on the road, and the black cadejo, a demon that follows drunks and sinners to lead them to their death. People who walk home late often tell of a large silent dog that walked beside them until they reached their door.':'Le cadejo est un grand chien aux yeux rouges lumineux et aux sabots de chèvre, qui apparaît aux personnes marchant seules la nuit. Il en existe deux : le cadejo blanc, gardien qui protège les voyageurs, et le cadejo noir, démon qui suit ivrognes et pécheurs pour les mener à la mort. Ceux qui rentrent tard racontent souvent qu’un grand chien silencieux les a escortés jusqu’à leur porte.',
'The white cadejo protects you from the black one. Never run from the black cadejo and never insult it: pray and walk steadily, and it may lose interest.':'Le cadejo blanc te protège du noir. Ne fuis jamais le cadejo noir et ne l’insulte pas : prie et continue d’avancer calmement, il pourrait se désintéresser de toi.',
'The ahuizotl is a water creature of Aztec legend, like a dog or an otter with smooth black fur, monkey-like hands, and an extra hand at the end of its tail. It lives in deep pools and lakes and grabs people at the water’s edge with its tail hand, pulling them under. Weeks later, their bodies float up without eyes, nails or teeth, which it likes to eat. Fray Bernardino de Sahagún recorded the legend in the 1500s.':'L’ahuizotl est une créature aquatique des légendes aztèques, semblable à un chien ou à une loutre au pelage noir et lisse. Il a des mains de singe et une main supplémentaire au bout de la queue. Il vit dans les lacs et les bassins profonds, attrape les gens au bord de l’eau avec sa queue et les entraîne sous la surface. Des semaines plus tard, leurs corps remontent sans yeux, ongles ni dents, qu’il aime manger. Fray Bernardino de Sahagún consigna cette légende au XVIe siècle.',
'The ahuizotl imitates a crying baby to lure people to the edge. Aztec priests said its victims belonged to the rain god Tlaloc and were buried with honour; stay away from the water when you hear a child crying.':'L’ahuizotl imite les pleurs d’un bébé pour attirer les gens au bord de l’eau. Les prêtres aztèques disaient que ses victimes appartenaient au dieu de la pluie Tlaloc et méritaient des funérailles honorables. Éloigne-toi de l’eau si tu entends un enfant pleurer.',
'La Llorona, ‘the Weeping Woman’, is the ghost of a beautiful woman named María who drowned her own children in a river in a moment of rage and despair, after the man she loved left her. Overcome with grief, she drowned herself too. She was not allowed into the afterlife until she found her children, so she wanders the rivers at night in a white dress, crying ‘¡Ay, mis hijos!’ — ‘Oh, my children!’':'La Llorona, « la Femme qui pleure », est le fantôme de María, une belle femme qui, dans un accès de rage et de désespoir après le départ de l’homme qu’elle aimait, noya ses enfants dans une rivière. Accablée de chagrin, elle se noya à son tour. Interdite d’au-delà tant qu’elle n’a pas retrouvé ses enfants, elle erre la nuit au bord des rivières, vêtue de blanc, en criant « ¡Ay, mis hijos! » — « Oh, mes enfants ! »',
'If her cry sounds far away, she is close; if it sounds close, she is far. She may take children who wander by the water at night: stay home after dark. Prayers and a rosary keep her away.':'Si ses pleurs semblent lointains, elle est proche ; s’ils semblent proches, elle est loin. Elle pourrait emporter les enfants qui s’approchent de l’eau la nuit : reste chez toi après la tombée du jour. Les prières et un chapelet l’éloignent.',
'The chupacabra, ‘goat-sucker’, is a modern legend. It began in Puerto Rico in 1995, when farmers found goats, sheep and chickens dead, with small holes in their necks and their blood drained. Witnesses described a creature about a metre tall with grey-green scaly skin, big red eyes and spines down its back, which hopped like a kangaroo. Soon the sightings spread to Mexico, Chile and the United States.':'Le chupacabra, le « suceur de chèvres », est une légende moderne. Elle commença à Porto Rico en 1995, lorsque des fermiers trouvèrent des chèvres, des moutons et des poulets morts, de petits trous au cou et vidés de leur sang. Des témoins décrivirent une créature d’environ un mètre, à la peau écailleuse gris-vert, aux grands yeux rouges et au dos hérissé d’épines, qui bondissait comme un kangourou. Les témoignages se répandirent bientôt au Mexique, au Chili et aux États-Unis.',
'The chupacabra hunts only at night and targets animals in pens. Strong fences, lights and dogs keep it away. Many ‘chupacabras’ that were caught turned out to be coyotes with mange.':'Le chupacabra chasse uniquement la nuit et s’en prend aux animaux dans les enclos. Des clôtures solides, des lumières et des chiens le tiennent à distance. Beaucoup de « chupacabras » capturés se sont révélés être des coyotes atteints de gale.',
'The sisimite is a giant, hairy creature of the Central American mountains, stronger than ten men, with feet that point backwards so that anyone following its tracks walks the wrong way. It lives in caves and kidnaps women to take as wives. In some Maya tales, the sisimite tries to learn the secret of fire from humans so it can rule them.':'Le sisimite est une créature géante et poilue des montagnes d’Amérique centrale, plus forte que dix hommes. Ses pieds sont tournés vers l’arrière : qui suit ses traces part dans la mauvaise direction. Il vit dans des grottes et enlève des femmes pour en faire ses épouses. Dans certains contes mayas, il tente de voler le secret du feu aux humains afin de les dominer.',
'Follow its tracks the opposite way, since its feet point backwards. It envies the secret of fire: keep a fire burning and it keeps its distance.':'Suis ses traces dans le sens opposé, car ses pieds sont tournés vers l’arrière. Il convoite le secret du feu : garde un feu allumé et il restera à distance.'
});
